{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.229,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "REAL NUMBERS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.885,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.137,
            0.651,
            0.17
          ],
          "content": "**So, we can find integers** r **and** $s \\left( \\neq 0 \\right)$ **such that** $\\left[ { \\sqrt { 2 } } = { \\frac { r } { s } } \\right.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.174,
            0.9,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "**Suppose** r **and** s **have a common factor other than 1. Then, we divide by the common factor to get** ${ \\sqrt { 2 } } = { \\frac { a } { b } }$ , where a and b **are coprime.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.238,
            0.235,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "**So,** $b   { \\sqrt { 2 } } \\; = a .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.267,
            0.868,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": "Squaring on both sides and rearranging, we get $2 b ^ { 2 } = a ^ { 2 }$ **. Therefore, 2 divides** $a ^ { 2 }$ Now, by Theorem 1.2, it follows that 2 divides a."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.318,
            0.518,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": "**So, we can write** $a = 2 c$ **for some integer** c."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.343,
            0.598,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": "**Substituting for** a, we get $2 b ^ { 2 } = 4 c ^ { 2 }$ , that is, $b ^ { 2 } = 2 c ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.367,
            0.897,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "**This means that 2 divides** $b ^ { 2 } ,$ **, and so 2 divides** b **(again using Theorem 1.2 with** $\\hat { p }   =   2 )$ **Therefore,** a **and** b **have at least 2 as a common factor.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.419,
            0.853,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "**But this contradicts the fact that** a **and** b **have no common factors other than 1.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.448,
            0.898,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "**This contradiction has arisen because of our incorrect assumption that** $\\sqrt { 2 }$ **is rational. So, we conclude that** $\\sqrt { 2 }$ **is irrational.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.514,
            0.494,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5 : Prove that** $\\sqrt { 3 }$ **is irrational.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.543,
            0.691,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "**Solution : Let us assume, to the contrary, that** $\\sqrt { 3 }$ **is rational.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.578,
            0.694,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "**That is, we can find integers** a **and** $b ( \\neq 0 )$ **such that** $\\left\\lceil { \\sqrt { 3 } } \\right. = { \\frac { a } { b } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.617,
            0.9,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": "**Suppose** a **and** b **have a common factor other than 1, then we can divide by the common factor, and assume that** a **and** b **are coprime.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.664,
            0.231,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "**So,** $b { \\sqrt { 3 } } = a$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.693,
            0.642,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "Squaring on both sides, and rearranging, we get $3 b ^ { 2 } = a ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.718,
            0.9,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "**Therefore,** $a ^ { 2 }$ **is divisible by 3, and by Theorem 1.2, it follows that** a **is also divisible by 3.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.763,
            0.518,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "**So, we can write** $\\tilde { a } = 3 c$ **for some integer** c."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.787,
            0.598,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": "**Substituting for** a, we get $3 b ^ { 2 } = 9 c ^ { 2 } ,$ , that is, $b ^ { 2 } = 3 c ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.813,
            0.901,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": "**This means that** $b ^ { 2 }$ **is divisible by 3, and so** b **is also divisible by 3 (using Theorem 1.2 with** $p = 3 )$ **.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.436,
